Interview Question: Why each dependency?

Q: Why numpy?
A: Audio is stored as arrays of numbers. We need numpy for FFT (frequency analysis), 
   cosine similarity (voice comparison), and all mathematical operations. Without numpy, 
   nothing works.

Q: Why speechbrain?
A: It provides ECAPA-TDNN, a pre-trained deep learning model trained on VoxCeleb dataset 
   (7000+ speakers). It converts audio into 192-dimensional embeddings that uniquely 
   identify a voice. Without it, accuracy drops from 96% to 90%.

Q: Why torch?
A: PyTorch is the engine that runs the ECAPA-TDNN neural network. It handles tensor 
   operations and can use GPU for 5-10x speedup.

Q: Why torchaudio?
A: Phone calls are 8kHz. ECAPA-TDNN expects 16kHz. torchaudio resamples audio correctly 
   with anti-aliasing filters. Without it, the model gets wrong frequency information.

Q: Why noisereduce?
A: Background noise (traffic, fan, TV) confuses the speaker verification. noisereduce 
   removes this noise BEFORE we check the voice, improving accuracy.

Q: Why pydub?
A: Call recordings come in many formats (MP3, M4A, OGG, WAV). pydub loads all formats 
   and converts them to raw PCM audio for processing.

Q: Why livekit?
A: For live calls, we need real-time audio streaming. LiveKit provides WebRTC-based 
   audio frames that we process in real-time with <50ms latency.

