Interview Q&A:

Q: Why do you buffer 500ms of audio before computing embeddings?
A: 20ms is too short for reliable speaker verification. Research shows:
   - 20ms: Error rate >40%
   - 100ms: Error rate ~25%
   - 500ms: Error rate ~8%
   - 1000ms: Error rate ~5%
   
   By buffering 25 chunks (25 × 20ms = 500ms), we get a 4x improvement in 
   embedding quality compared to single-chunk processing.

Q: What is cosine similarity and why do you use it?
A: Cosine similarity measures the angle between two vectors:
   
   cos(A, B) = (A · B) / (|A| × |B|)
   
   Since we L2-normalize all embeddings (|A| = |B| = 1), this simplifies to 
   the dot product:
   
   cos(A, B) = A · B = Σ(aᵢ × bᵢ) for i=1 to 192
   
   Interpretation:
   - cos = 1.0: Identical voice
   - cos = 0.7-0.9: Same person, different recording
   - cos = 0.5-0.7: Similar voice (maybe same person)
   - cos = 0.3-0.5: Different person
   - cos < 0.3: Very different voice
   
   Our threshold is 0.52, meaning we accept voices with ≥52% similarity.

Q: What is the threshold and how is it determined?
A: The threshold (default 0.52) is the minimum cosine similarity for a voice 
   to be accepted as the customer. It is determined by:
   1. Base value: 0.52 (empirically tested)
   2. Adaptive calibration: Adjusts based on real call data
   3. SNR adaptation: Relaxes in noisy conditions
   4. Fail-open: Lowers if too many rejections occur
   
   A similarity of 0.52 means the voice must be at least 52% similar to the 
   customer's profile.

Q: What is SFM (Spectral Flatness Measure)?
A: SFM measures how "flat" or "peaked" the frequency spectrum is.
   - Low SFM (< 0.06): Tonal/peaked → Music, humming, tones
   - Medium SFM (0.06-0.88): Mixed → Human voice
   - High SFM (> 0.88): Flat → White noise, static
   
   We use it to reject non-voice audio (music, noise) before doing expensive 
   embedding comparison.

Q: What is the pitch bonus/penalty?
A: Pitch (F0) is the fundamental frequency of the voice. Men typically have 
   F0 of 85-180 Hz, women 165-255 Hz. If the incoming pitch is close to the 
   customer's enrolled pitch, we add a small bonus (+0.05). If it is very 
   different, we add a penalty (-0.05). This helps during overlap when the 
   embedding similarity is uncertain.

Q: What is SNR-adaptive threshold?
A: SNR (Signal-to-Noise Ratio) measures how much louder the voice is compared 
   to background noise. In noisy conditions (low SNR), the embedding extraction 
   is less reliable, so we relax the threshold to avoid false rejections. In 
   clean conditions (high SNR), we use the strict threshold.
   
   SNR > 15dB: Clean → Strict threshold (0.52)
   SNR 5-15dB: Moderate → Partially relaxed (0.48-0.52)
   SNR < 5dB: Noisy → Relaxed threshold (0.44)


5.4 _build_profile() - Detailed Explanation
What it does:

Builds customer voice profile from enrollment audio
Uses MEDIAN instead of MEAN (robust to outliers)
Detects contamination (multiple speakers)
Builds pitch profile

Why MEDIAN instead of MEAN?

Example: 5 embedding windows from enrollment

Window 1: [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]  ← Customer
Window 2: [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]  ← Customer
Window 3: [0.9, 0.8, 0.7]  ← Background person! (contamination)
Window 4: [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]  ← Customer
Window 5: [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]  ← Customer

MEAN:   [0.26, 0.32, 0.38]  ← Pulled toward contaminator ❌
MEDIAN: [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]     ← Ignores the outlier ✅

Median is robust to contamination!

Contamination Detection Algorithm:

1. Extract embeddings from multiple windows of enrollment audio
2. Compute pairwise cosine similarity between all windows
3. If all windows are from the same person:
   Mean inter-window similarity: 0.84 (high) ✅
4. If a second person spoke during enrollment:
   Mean inter-window similarity: 0.57 (low) ❌
5. If mean < 0.60, flag contamination


Interview Q&A:

Q: How does contamination detection work?
A: We extract embeddings from multiple windows of the enrollment audio. Then we 
   compute pairwise similarities between all windows. If all windows are from the 
   same person, similarities should be high (>0.7). If there is a second person, 
   some windows will have low similarity to others, bringing the mean down. If 
   mean inter-window similarity < 0.60, we flag contamination.

Q: Why do you use overlapping windows for enrollment?
A: Overlapping windows (50% overlap) ensure we capture all voice characteristics. 
   A 300ms window with 150ms hop means every part of the enrollment audio is 
   analyzed by at least 2 windows. This gives a more comprehensive profile.

